# Provenance for s4cov_hd_format_2026-08-04.tsv
# (KB-36's >=1080p band at the format axes it did not sweep; the band
#  1440..2160 at speed >= 1; the crop straddle at `is_4k_or_larger`; and the
#  speed-0 >=1080p localizer that found KB-38.)

base commit      : 38b79ee (origin/main) + this session's partial-SB hbd gate
host             : Darwin 26.5.2 arm64 / Apple M-series
profile          : test-fast (opt-level 3, debug-assertions + overflow-checks ON)
dispatch         : default (archmage SIMD tokens live)
conformance dir  : conformance/data (copy of the primary checkout's fetched corpus)
wall             : 26 s + 119 s + 431 s + 35 s + 648 s + 629 s = ~31 min

command          : cargo test --profile test-fast -p zenav1-aom-bench \
                     --test s4cov_hd_format_axis -- --ignored --nocapture --test-threads=1

WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
  KB-36 closed the `is_1080p_or_larger` arm of `default_min_partition_size`
  on a grid that is ONE CELL WIDE in format — bd8 4:2:0 SB64 cq24 — and its
  own note says so. KB-36 is also the precedent for why that matters: its arm
  is one SPEED wide, so a speed sweep at any size under 1080 and a size sweep
  at any speed but 6 were both green with the arm missing.

RESULT BY ARM
  above_1080p_format_axis   14/14 MATCH  4:4:4 / 4:2:2 / mono / SB128 / cq5 /
                                         cq40 / cq63, each at 1920x1072 AND
                                         1920x1080, at cpu 6 (the ONLY speed
                                         where KB-36's arm is observable)
  above_1080p_hbd            6/8  MATCH  bd10 + bd12 at 1920x1072/1080, cpu 7
                                         all match; the two cpu-0 rows at 1080
                                         diverge and are KB-38's band (which
                                         reaches bd8 — see below), not an
                                         hbd-specific finding
  band_1440_2160            18/18 MATCH  1920x1920 + 2560x1600 x cpu 1..9 —
                                         strictly between KB-36's 1440 ceiling
                                         and the 2160 `is_4k_or_larger` arm
  crop_straddle_4k           2/2  MATCH  2154x2160 vs 2160x2160 at cpu 5
  speed0_1080p_PRE           9/12 MATCH  the localizer, BEFORE KB-38's arm
  speed0_1080p_POST          9/12 MATCH  ... and after it (deltas move, the
                                         cells do not close — see KB-38)

THE 2160 STRADDLE COST 35 SECONDS, NOT 25-30 MINUTES
  The coverage queue and `s4cov_crop_format_axis.rs`'s own residual note both
  costed this arm from KB-19's speed-0 cell (C ~26 s / port ~195 s). Measured
  here: 20 s and 15 s for the two encodes, ~50x cheaper. The reason is that
  `is_4k_or_larger` is speed-UNconditional (KB-19), so it is observable at any
  speed 0..5 — from speed 6 the `is_1080p_or_larger` arm sets the same field to
  the same value for a frame this large (KB-36), and from speed 7
  `set_allintra` sets it framesize-independently (:570). The estimate assumed
  the speed the arm was FOUND at rather than the cheapest speed it is
  OBSERVABLE at. Same razor as KB-28's: 2154 mi-aligns UP to 2160, so the pair
  agrees under the mi reading and disagrees under the crop reading.

KB-38 — WHAT THE SPEED-0 LOCALIZER FOUND, AND WHAT IT DID NOT CLOSE
  Predicate (stated before the run, falsifiable on one cell):
    speed == 0 && AOMMIN(w,h) >= 1080 && base_qindex <= 108
  Nine of twelve rows obey it exactly. `1920x1072` (eight pixels of the same
  content) is byte-exact at both bit depths and both quantizers; `1280x720` is
  byte-exact at both; `1920x1080 cq32` is byte-exact at bd8.
  The arm (speed_features.c:2926-2935, nested inside KB-22's own >=720p arm)
  sets five fields the port carries and four of which are INTRA-live. It was
  omitted under a comment reading "all inter-only, and the port carries no
  field for them" — both halves false (playbook §9).
  Porting it MOVED the two cq24 cells (bd8 -536 -> -726 B, bd10 +483 -> +408)
  and closed NEITHER, and a twelfth row diverges that the predicate does not
  explain at all: bd10 1920x1080 cq32, -8 B, whose bd8 twin matches. So at
  least one further root sits at (speed 0, >= 1080p). Recorded as a
  self-promoting pin (`speed0_1080p_band_map_is_pinned`), NOT as a closure.

BITE PROOFS (playbook §1 — asymmetry, not just failure)
  format axis + band     stub KB-36's arm (`if false && speed >= 6 &&
                         min_dim >= 1080`). Format axis: 6/7 >=1080p rows
                         diverge (+97/-254/+382/+249/-638/+10 B) and ALL 7
                         <1080p twins stay byte-exact. Band: EXACTLY the two
                         --cpu-used 6 rows diverge (-17 / +167 B), all 16
                         other speed rows byte-exact. The 7th format row
                         (cq63) is inert on both sides — at base_qindex 252
                         the search never descends to 4x4, so the field has
                         nothing to clamp; kept as quantizer coverage, not a
                         bite cell.
  2160 crop straddle     make the arm read the MI extent instead of the crop
                         (KB-28's root at this boundary): 2154x2160 diverges
                         -2270 B while 2160x2160 stays byte-exact. A pair
                         that both diverged would prove nothing about the
                         crop read.
  Both reverted; `git diff` clean on the perturbed file before commit.

DISPATCH MODES
  Default dispatch: every arm above. `AOM_FORCE_SCALAR=1`: the whole
  `s4cov_hd_format_axis` file re-run with `--include-ignored` (see the
  scalar-leg note in the landing commit).

